00:08:31.797 --> 00:08:36.607
Immersion cooling takes the heat off by sitting the server in a dielectric bath, and the mechanism

00:08:36.607 --> 00:08:40.647
is worth taking apart because the tank keeps losing bids it should win on physics.

00:08:41.247 --> 00:08:44.767
Heat moves from the part into liquid, then to a heat exchanger.

00:08:45.227 --> 00:08:47.547
In the single phase version, the fluid never boils.

00:08:48.147 --> 00:08:52.727
Pump circulate it, the hull does not have to be drawn around airflow, and the cooling

00:08:52.727 --> 00:08:55.727
loop does not consume water the way an evaporative plant does.

00:08:56.427 --> 00:09:01.087
Data center knowledge notes that dropping fans and airflow dependencies also remove some

00:09:01.087 --> 00:09:04.347
operational risk, and that the method is decades old.

00:09:04.847 --> 00:09:08.967
In 2026, it is still not how large operators cool production floors.

00:09:09.587 --> 00:09:14.707
What gets announced is a pilot or a proof, including Microsoft's experiment earlier this decade.

00:09:15.267 --> 00:09:22.707
The liquid approach that is actually forecast to scale is direct to chip, coolant and

00:09:22.707 --> 00:09:28.287
a normal rack, the install price is the veto. Data center knowledge cites about $1 million

00:09:28.287 --> 00:09:34.967
per megawatt of IT load for an immersion system, against $650,000 per megawatt for direct

00:09:34.967 --> 00:09:41.467
to chip. That is $350,000 of extra capital on every megawatt before the IT load is live,

00:09:41.967 --> 00:09:47.287
which is exactly the moment operators are trying to energize fast. The decade-long bill looks

00:09:47.287 --> 00:09:50.327
